{
  "dom_id": "step-q340151",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "Find the value of the constant $b$ that makes the function $h(x)$ below continuous at $x=1.$\n\n$$\nh(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{bx}, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "At $x=1,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}{e}^{bx} \\\\  & ={e}^{b(1)} \\\\  & ={e}^{b},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(3{x}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =3(1)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}h(x) \\\\ {e}^{b} & =1 \\\\ {e}^{b} & ={e}^{0} \\\\ b & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
